# Practical: Git/GitHub — Add, Push, Pull & Merge

This practical continues from your earlier **Git and GitHub** work. We will perform all four operations:

```text
ADD → PUSH → PULL → MERGE
```

We'll use a simple project called `git-merge-demo`.

---

## 1. Create a project

Open **Command Prompt / PowerShell / Terminal**.

```bash
mkdir git-merge-demo
cd git-merge-demo
```

Create a file called:

```text
index.html
```

Put this inside:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Git Merge Demo</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Hello GitHub</h1>
</body>
</html>
```

---

# 2. Initialize Git

Run:

```bash
git init
```

Expected:

```text
Initialized empty Git repository...
```

Check the status:

```bash
git status
```

You should see `index.html` as an untracked file.

---

# 3. Git ADD

The `git add` command puts changes into the **staging area**.

Run:

```bash
git add index.html
```

Or, to add everything:

```bash
git add .
```

Now check:

```bash
git status
```

You should see:

```text
Changes to be committed:
    new file: index.html
```

### Remember

```text
Working Directory
       ↓
    git add
       ↓
Staging Area
```

---

# 4. COMMIT the changes

Although your practical title focuses on Add, Push, Pull and Merge, you need a commit before pushing.

Run:

```bash
git commit -m "Initial commit"
```

Expected:

```text
[main xxxxxxx] Initial commit
 1 file changed
```

---

# 5. Create a GitHub repository

Go to:

[GitHub](https://github.com/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Create a new repository named:

```text
git-merge-demo
```

For this exercise, you can make it **Public**.

If you're connecting your existing local repository, don't create another README initially.

---

# 6. Connect local Git to GitHub

Copy your repository URL from GitHub.

It will look like:

```text
https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/git-merge-demo.git
```

Run:

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/git-merge-demo.git
```

Check:

```bash
git remote -v
```

You should see your GitHub repository.

---

# 7. Push to GitHub

Rename your branch to `main`:

```bash
git branch -M main
```

Now push:

```bash
git push -u origin main
```

Your project should now appear on GitHub.

### Remember

```text
Local computer
      ↓
   git push
      ↓
   GitHub
```

---

# 8. Create a feature branch

Now we'll create a separate branch so we can demonstrate **merge**.

Run:

```bash
git switch -c feature
```

Check your branches:

```bash
git branch
```

You should see:

```text
* feature
  main
```

The `*` shows your current branch.

---

# 9. Make a change

Open `index.html`.

Change:

```html
<h1>Hello GitHub</h1>
```

to:

```html
<h1>Hello GitHub - Feature Branch</h1>
```

Save it.

---

# 10. ADD the change

Run:

```bash
git add index.html
```

Check:

```bash
git status
```

---

# 11. COMMIT the change

Run:

```bash
git commit -m "Update heading in feature branch"
```

Now the change is saved in the `feature` branch.

---

# 12. Push the feature branch

Run:

```bash
git push -u origin feature
```

Now GitHub will contain two branches:

```text
main
feature
```

You can see them from your GitHub repository's **Branches** section.

---

# 13. Pull from GitHub

Now let's understand **PULL**.

First switch back to `main`:

```bash
git switch main
```

Run:

```bash
git pull origin main
```

This downloads the latest changes from the GitHub `main` branch and integrates them into your local `main`.

### Remember

```text
GitHub
   ↓
git pull
   ↓
Local repository
```

---

# 14. Merge the feature branch

Now we're going to merge the changes from `feature` into `main`.

Make sure you are on `main`:

```bash
git switch main
```

Check:

```bash
git branch
```

You should see:

```text
  feature
* main
```

Now merge:

```bash
git merge feature
```

You should get something similar to:

```text
Updating xxxxxxx..xxxxxxx
Fast-forward
 index.html | 2 +-
```

The feature changes are now part of `main`.

---

# 15. Push the merged code to GitHub

Run:

```bash
git push origin main
```

Now GitHub's `main` branch contains the changes from `feature`.

---

# 16. Verify the merge

Run:

```bash
git log --oneline --all
```

You should see your commits.

You can also open your GitHub repository and select the **main** branch.

The updated `index.html` should be there.

---

