# If you get a merge conflict

A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically decide which change to keep.

For example, suppose `main` has:

```html
<h1>Hello from Main</h1>
```

and `feature` has:

```html
<h1>Hello from Feature</h1>
```

Git may mark the file like this:

```text
<<<<<<< HEAD
<h1>Hello from Main</h1>
=======
<h1>Hello from Feature</h1>
>>>>>>> feature
```

You manually edit it to the version you want, for example:

```html
<h1>Hello from Main and Feature</h1>
```

Then:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Resolve merge conflict"
```

Finally:

```bash
git push origin main
```

